# Web Image Converter & Tagging Platform

This plan outlines the architecture for a completely new, web-based version of the Image Converter tool. The goal is to create a robust, easily hostable React application with a sleek User Interface, robust individual file overrides, and the native capabilities to inject FastGeotagger-supported EXIF metadata purely in the browser.

## User Review Required

> [!WARNING]  
> Because browsers do not have any native capability to inject raw EXIF metadata into PNG `eXIf` chunks, I will be engineering a custom JavaScript data stream encoder that perfectly packages the EXIF signatures we reverse-engineered from FastGeotagger without needing Python or a Node backend!
> 
> **Does the React workflow work for you?** I will be using **Vite + React** to build this tool, as handling state for dozens of individual images + global settings can get messy in pure Vanilla HTML. Once built, I can give you the compiled, production-ready `html/js/css` folder which you can upload instantly to any website directly!

## Proposed Architecture

This system will utilize a modern Web framework designed for high-performance interaction.

### Frontend Technologies (Vite + React)
- **UI Framework**: React. Enables us to easily create a grid of thumbnails, track individual settings for each image, and seamlessly manage the global checkboxes you requested.
- **Styling**: Pure CSS with modern aesthetics (glassmorphism overlays on thumbnails, dark-mode themes, and subtle animations).
- **Processing Engine**: The HTML5 `<canvas>` will inherently decode and draw your selected `Webp` and `AVIF` items, allowing us to perfectly apply Resizing if needed, before instantly converting them into a `.png` Blob.
- **Metadata Engine**: `piexifjs` will be used to generate the precise binary tables, and a custom buffer-injection function will pry open the generated PNG to weave the `eXIf` chunk securely inside, right next to your XMP metadata.

